==Italian==
===Toponym===
'''Cesina''' (''plural'' '''Cesine''')  derives from the Latin '''''caesi''''', from the Latin verb caedere to which the Langobard suffix -'''na''' is added.<br/>
'''Toponym''' of [[Langobard]] or late Latin origin, used in southern [[Italy]], especially in [[Campania]].<br/>
It was probably introduced with the creation of the Duchy of Benevento by the Lombards around 590 AD.<br/>
It defined wooded area where to cut wood.<br/>
They are precisely defined in the '''I tome of Antiquitates Italicae Medii Aevi, year 1005 AD''', column 183 written by '''Ludovico Antonio Muratori''' in 1738-43.<br/>
Then define as '''Silva cædua''' ([[Latin]]) in the '''Du Cange''', et al., '''Glossarium mediae et infimae Latinitatis, Niort: L. Favre''', 1883–1887 (10 vol.).<br/>
These wooded areas were often deforested to make room for urban settlement and cultivated countryside.<br/>